WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:05.520
With a runtime that exceeds the four-hour mark,&nbsp;
Zack Snyder's Justice League differs wildly from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.520 --> 00:00:10.960
the 2017 theatrical version of Justice League,&nbsp;
which was comprised heavily of footage and scenes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.960 --> 00:00:17.120
that were either reshot, added, or rewritten by&nbsp;
Avengers director Joss Whedon. The 2017 cut was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.120 --> 00:00:21.840
very different from what Snyder had originally&nbsp;
intended the film to be, and as a result,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.840 --> 00:00:26.960
many fans have taken to regarding the Snyder Cut&nbsp;
as the one and only "real" version of the film.

00:00:26.960 --> 00:00:31.920
Of course, DC fans weren't the only ones happy to&nbsp;
see the release of Zack Snyder's Justice League.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.920 --> 00:00:36.080
The fan campaign that ultimately led&nbsp;
to the completion of the Snyder Cut was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.080 --> 00:00:39.760
boosted over the years by comments&nbsp;
from several Justice League stars&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.760 --> 00:00:43.120
who expressed their desire to see the&nbsp;
original version of the film released.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.680 --> 00:00:48.720
Snyder himself also appears to be visibly relieved&nbsp;
and grateful to have gotten the chance to return&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.720 --> 00:00:54.400
to the project — even if it also marks the&nbsp;
end of his time in the DCEU once and for all.

00:00:54.400 --> 00:00:58.080
It looks like it's not just DC&nbsp;
fans or members of the film's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.080 --> 00:01:02.080
creative team who were excited to&nbsp;
see the updated, four hour marathon.

00:01:02.080 --> 00:01:03.280
"We have to stand together."

00:01:05.600 --> 00:01:09.440
Jeffrey Dean Morgan shared his reaction&nbsp;
to the film on Twitter this past weekend,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.440 --> 00:01:14.800
with the Walking Dead actor adding to the growing&nbsp;
pile of online praise for the film by tweeting,

00:01:14.800 --> 00:01:19.520
"HELL YES. #snydercut congratulations&nbsp;
my friend. You did us proud."

00:01:19.520 --> 00:01:23.280
Despite having no role in the film&nbsp;
itself, Morgan was one of several&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.280 --> 00:01:27.920
notable celebrities who tweeted about it last&nbsp;
week. Avengers: Endgame directors Anthony and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.920 --> 00:01:32.880
Joe Russo also publicly tweeted their excitement&nbsp;
and support for the film ahead of its premiere.

00:01:32.880 --> 00:01:37.760
Of course, Morgan himself does have some&nbsp;
connections to the DC Universe, even if he has no&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.760 --> 00:01:42.880
role in the actual film. The actor has a history&nbsp;
with Snyder, having appeared as The Comedian in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.880 --> 00:01:49.200
Snyder's 2009 film adaptation of Watchmen. Morgan&nbsp;
also had a small role as Thomas Wayne — the late&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.200 --> 00:01:55.280
father of Ben Affleck's Bruce Wayne — in 2016's&nbsp;
Batman v Superman: Dawn of Justice. Right now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.280 --> 00:02:01.120
the actor stars as Negan on AMC's The Walking&nbsp;
Dead and isn't currently slated to appear in any&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.120 --> 00:02:06.240
of Snyder's upcoming films, but it's clear that he&nbsp;
still feels a strong connection to the filmmaker.

00:02:06.240 --> 00:02:10.080
Morgan's reaction also comes at the&nbsp;
same time that many fans are still&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.080 --> 00:02:15.600
calling for his presence in the DCEU to be&nbsp;
revived in a future DC comics adaptation.

00:02:16.320 --> 00:02:20.960
Jeffrey Dean Morgan's role in Batman v&nbsp;
Superman: Dawn of Justice was, admittedly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.960 --> 00:02:26.000
brief. The actor only appeared as Thomas Wayne&nbsp;
in one of the film's opening flashback scenes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.000 --> 00:02:32.720
and he hasn't been seen again in any other DCEU&nbsp;
film. But that hasn't stopped DC comics fans from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.720 --> 00:02:36.960
publicly calling for Morgan to be brought&nbsp;
back as Thomas Wayne in a solo Flash film.

00:02:37.520 --> 00:02:42.720
Specifically, DC fans have been open about their&nbsp;
desire to see Morgan's take on Thomas Wayne return&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.720 --> 00:02:47.280
in a possible adaptation of the Flashpoint&nbsp;
storyline from the comics, which saw The Flash&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.280 --> 00:02:52.480
arrive in an alternate reality where Thomas Wayne&nbsp;
became Batman after watching his son, Bruce, die.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.120 --> 00:02:57.760
Unfortunately, despite the overwhelming&nbsp;
fan support for Morgan's DCEU return,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.760 --> 00:03:03.120
it seems unlikely that the actor will be&nbsp;
reprising his role as Thomas Wayne anytime soon.

00:03:03.120 --> 00:03:06.640
Instead, Michael Keaton is currently&nbsp;
set to return as Batman in director&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.640 --> 00:03:11.760
Andy Muschietti's Flash movie, while Morgan's&nbsp;
time in the DCEU appears to be up. At least,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.760 --> 00:03:16.320
that's what the actor told Comicbook.com last&nbsp;
year in response to Keaton's return, saying,

00:03:16.320 --> 00:03:19.040
"Michael Keaton swooped in and took my gig. Naw,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.040 --> 00:03:23.520
I think that's super cool. Ever since Zack&nbsp;
Snyder walked away, my whole kind of bit in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.520 --> 00:03:28.640
that world has walked away with him. However,&nbsp;
there's always a chat going on. So, we'll see!"

00:03:28.640 --> 00:03:32.480
In other words, it looks like Jeffrey&nbsp;
Dean Morgan's screen time in the DCEU&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.480 --> 00:03:36.800
may end up being confined to just a few&nbsp;
moments. However, it's clear that he's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.800 --> 00:03:41.120
not letting that stop him from continuing&nbsp;
to show support for Zack Snyder's work.

